module gunbc.plans.names_without_imports

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.markdown_helpers { h2, p, li, ul, ol, quote }

fn names_without_imports_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**This plan is written as an intention** (`gunbc.design_argument::plans_are_intentions`). The work items live under the `namespace-unique-on-chain` project in `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_authority` and name this intention; this plan does not list them."),
      p(text: "**Grounding:** `gunbc.design_argument::single_authority` (one name, one way to decide it; the name-to-path mapping runs one way), `gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration` (the old authority ends in one motion at its root), `gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization`, `gunbc.design_argument::closed_grounded_substrate`, `gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility`."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "1. Intention"),
    p(text: "**What:** every name in a `.dag` program is decided one canonical way, by declaration in scope or by qualified path, through one binding kernel, and the import concept is gone from the language: no import grammar, no import closure deciding the compiled population, no import retry, no global pool of bare names, and no second deciding mechanism in the seed."),
    p(text: "**Why (operator, 2026-11-16):** imports cause a lot of problems, and by the time v2 is accepted they must be gone for good. The problems are on record: a file that declares any import silently gets no bare edges, so the compiled population is decided by accident (`namespace-v2-import-completion`); the symbol index is built from a tree-wide census while the compiled population is the import closure, two answers to what a name sees; and a name answered two ways by two code paths is this project's own red control. The operator also recorded that the project has been consistently mishandled, mostly by the operator: it has been re-scoped at least four times (the staged XL migration program withdrawn 2026-11-10, import-grammar deletion withdrawn 2026-11-08, re-admitted 2026-11-16), so this plan fixes the intention first and lets the realization be redrawn under it."),
    p(text: "**Three mechanisms answer a name today, and this intention leaves one.** (a) A declaration reached by lexical scope, an explicit import or a qualified path, the 2026-11-08 binding rule. (b) The resolver's four stopgap tables (`v2.compiler.resolve` `AmbientBindingTable`: the kernel type-spelling table, the kernel's canonical symbols, the std.primitives last tier, the collection roster), each recorded per occurrence as `BoundAmbient` and driven to deletion by the ambient-name census under the 2026-10-15 ruling that there are no built-ins and no prelude (`cross-module-declarations`'s boundary; the private ledger of gunb-ai/gunbc-private#289 counted 201 ambient-name rows on 2026-11-08). (c) The v1 seed's own built-ins, which answer the same sources a second way (`gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::an_unimported_bare_name_binds_differently_per_realization`). The ambient waves now landing (string_length, substring, char_at into `std.host_text`; fold, flat_map, first, last into `std.algebra`) are durable under this intention, because every name needs one declared home whatever reaches it; what is not durable is any investment in the reaching mechanism of (a), an import list that binds or an import retry, since the cut deletes it. The waves therefore continue, scoped to the native-seven closure first (161 of the compiler's 273 modules, by gunb-ai/gunbc-private#289's measurement), and the import-list machinery is frozen."),
    p(text: "**Done when** (each assessed against the tree unless marked as the operator's judgement):"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The v2 grammar has no import production, and a `.dag` file containing an import line refuses at parse with a located diagnostic naming the qualified-path spelling that replaces it."),
      li(text: "Every name reference carries its declaration (`declaration-identity-switchover`), decided by the one kernel handed its candidates (`namespace-binding-kernel`), with genuinely ambiguous names settled by discharge, not by order of loading (`namespace-ambiguity-discharge`)."),
      li(text: "Each file's dependencies are derived from what its names resolve to, so the compiled population is a consequence of references and never of an authored list (`namespace-reference-derived-closure`)."),
      li(text: "The legacy deciding mechanisms are at zero: name-string matching, parent-chain walking, the bare-name pool, the import retry, and the seed's own none/one/many mirror (`namespace-legacy-choice-retirement`'s handback)."),
      li(text: "`native-production-admission` consumes this: v2 is not admitted as the production route while any of the above is open (`gunbc.design_ruling::v2_landing_waits_on_the_namespace_program`, `gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_deleted_before_v2_is_accepted`)."),
      li(text: "Operator's judgement: the corpus reads better without imports than with them; a migration that merely moved the import lists into longer spellings at every reference has not achieved this."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "2. The backward structure, read at e346a2c89daa"),
    p(text: "The project's eleven members declare no edges among themselves, so this is read from their texts. Each layer states what must already be true for the one above it."),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**The goal.** No import grammar, and the compiled population derived from references. This is the last cut, because deleting the grammar before every name binds without it would refuse the whole corpus at once."),
      li(text: "**Just before it.** The legacy deciding mechanisms are deleted (`namespace-legacy-choice-retirement`), which is possible only when every ordinary consumer already uses the one rule."),
      li(text: "**Before that.** Every reference carries its declaration (`declaration-identity-switchover`), which needs the kernel (`namespace-binding-kernel`), exact occurrence identity (`namespace-occurrence-transport`), what a name can see from where it is written (`namespace-structural-observations`), dependencies derived from references (`namespace-reference-derived-closure`, with its producer bounded by `namespace-reference-derived-producer-bounded`), and the genuinely-many cases settled (`namespace-ambiguity-discharge`). `namespace-v2-import-completion`'s question (does an import list bind?) is answered by this intention: it does not; the import list is deleted, and in-scope is the switch-over's single definition."),
      li(text: "**Why name debt lands green today.** The required job has no native resolve step (gunb-ai/gunbc-private#289), so a landing that leaves a name ambient or unhomed passes CI and the next native-seven run finds it; site repairs were about twelve percent of migration commits and nothing held the line after a class-level fix. The operator ruled on 2026-11-16 that no ratchet is built for this (`gunbc.design_ruling::no_ratchets_names_bind_now_and_meaning_migrates_later`); what holds the line is a simple falsifiable reading used with judgment, section 6, until native resolve over the compiler closures refuses a bare or ambiguous name outright."),
      li(text: "**The scale, measured.** The 2026-11-16 parse sweep counts 10343 modules and about 292,000 import members. Every one of those lines is rewritten or made unnecessary by this program, so the realization is a generated rewrite through one generator over the whole corpus, never a hand migration, and it lands as one cut at the root with the generator as its offline differential oracle (`gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration`)."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Today's frontier**, read the same way: the kernel, occurrence transport and structural observations are the roots; the bounded producer is gated on gunb-ai/gunbc#11683 and gunb-ai/gunbc#11574 by its own text; `declaration-provider-outcomes` is also a parent of `native-seven` and is the member whose landing moves both intentions at once."),
    h2(text: "3. What this intention does not gate"),
    p(text: "`native-seven` and every landing before production admission. The 2026-11-08 ruling that native-seven depends only on its own closure stands (`gunbc.design_ruling::n7_requires_only_its_own_closure`), and imports remain the binding mechanism until the cut lands (`gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_the_terminal_name_mechanism`, whose withdrawal of grammar deletion is superseded but whose binding rule is not). The two intentions meet at `native-production-admission`."),
    h2(text: "4. Open questions"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**The qualified-path spelling without imports.** A file that names a foreign declaration today spells `decl` after importing it; without imports it spells the declaration's path. Whether the path is the full module path or a declared shorter form is a language design decision the kernel must make once, and it is the first thing a realization has to settle because the generated rewrite depends on it."),
      li(text: "**The interlock milestone.** `NamespaceGrammarRetired` was superseded on 2026-11-08 and is no longer a prerequisite in `gunbc.compiler_frontend_program_interlock`. Re-admitting it as a prerequisite of `SelfHostSeedRetirement` (or of production admission) is a realization act on that carrier, owed by whoever picks this program up, and not done in the plan change that recorded the ruling."),
      li(text: "**The seed.** The v1 seed parses imports and is semantics-frozen under a purpose test (`gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing`). Whether the seed learns the new spelling or the cut waits for the seed's retirement is an ordering decision between this intention and `gunbc.plans.v2_self_host`; the ruling that namespace gates production admission, not native-seven, is the only ordering fixed so far."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "5. Observation, 2026-11-16: the ambient ledger re-measured"),
    p(text: "The operator asked whether the waves had cleared eight of the ledger's rows. Measured with the ledger's own producer (gunb-ai/gunbc-private#289 `ambient_sweep.py`: a bare site is a call or pipe of a builtin-universe name in a file that neither imports nor declares it, a grep proxy and not the resolver's census) re-run against main at 0b4e665 and joined to the ledger by name, 156 rows join."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Bare sites across the joined rows: 110,228 on 2026-11-08, 117,840 today.** 53 names grew, 19 shrank, one reached zero (`repeat`, two sites). The corpus adds bare sites faster than the waves remove them: `concat` alone added about 2,500 in eight days."),
      li(text: "**Homing versus site migration are different work, and only the first landed.** 30 of the joined names now have a declared home under std or extdeps (string_length, substring, char_at, map_insert, empty_map, list_push, fold_list and others). Their call sites did not move: string_length 869 to 951 sites, substring 778 to 779, char_at 148 to 164. A home with no caller importing it is a declaration nothing consumes until the sweep that rewrites the sites runs."),
      li(text: "**Inside the native-seven closure** (161 modules by import edges) there are 1,514 bare sites today, and 35 of the 37 ledger names scoped to that closure still have at least one. The largest are the roster-bound higher-order names (fold 617, any 75, filter 62, map 30) and the template-bound pair (concat 212, count 118), which bind natively today through the collection roster and so are not refusals; the names that do refuse are the host-text and map families (discriminant 82, split 38, first 33, starts_with 28, join 25, skip 21, string_length 19, replace 18)."),
      li(text: "**The universe is over-broad.** The ledger's 97 rest-of-corpus rows are mostly host-realized instrument functions with one or two callers (compile_dag_*, doc_graph_*, observed_*), in the universe only because they are exported from the seed's runtime; 36 rows have no callers at all. The names that matter to this intention are the 46 native-seven rows, the five roster-bound higher-order names and the three remaining table arms."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Reading.** Eight of two hundred is the wrong frame in both directions: by homes, about thirty names have a declaration; by sites, essentially none has been cleared, and the total is rising. One name at a time, one PR per home and a hand-picked sweep of a few files each (the Wave A2 and D3 pattern) does not converge against a corpus adding thousands of sites a week. That is the measured case for section 2's generated rewrite as one cut. Not for a ratchet: the operator ruled those out the same day, and the number is watched by the reading in section 6 instead."),
    h2(text: "6. Observation, 2026-11-16: binding bare names to their one known declaration, read by a simple grep"),
    p(text: "The operator's preferred instrument: a deliberately simple reading, used with judgment and falsified by hand, kept with the session rather than enrolled anywhere. For each module it lists every call head the file neither declares nor imports and how many production declarations (fn, data, type, concept, enum under dag and src/v2, test and fixture modules excluded) carry that name. First falsification: the first version counted `fn` and `if` as call heads, counted lambda parameters called as functions, and called `join` unique because one test witness declares a `join`; all three were corrected before the numbers below."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Native-seven closure, 161 modules, bare call heads:** 110 sites of 13 names bind to exactly one production declaration today with no import (decl_ref 23, string_length 19, substring 12, map_insert 12, skip 11, length 10, empty_map 6, char_at 5, string_contains 5); 6 sites of one name are ambiguous (`contains`: std.algebra against gunbc.instruments.router_proxy_falsifier); 1,229 sites of 61 names have NO declaration anywhere (fold 586, concat 211, count 114, any 45, filter 41, starts_with 28, first 28, split 27, map 20, replace 18, to_string 13, code_point 10, and a tail of one to six sites each). The roster-bound seven bind natively today without a declaration; the other 54 are the names that still need a home before native-seven can pass, and they are the whole of step one."),
      li(text: "**Would deleting the import lines lose a binding?** Of the native-seven closure's 6,270 import members, 3,902 name a declaration unique in production, 2,300 name a variant constructor the reading does not index, and 68 would become ambiguous without the import: String (v2.std.node against v2.std.text) 22, Int (std.integer against v2.std.integer) 14, contains 9, SourceSpan, TerminationProof, tokenize, IntegerOverflow. Over all 5,910 production modules: 149,854 members, 1,617 ambiguous (about one percent), led by FilePath (std.types against v2.extdeps.file_system) 292, String 176, Int 132, Unit 73, UInt8, PositiveInt. Nearly every collision is a dag std declaration re-declared under src/v2/std, which the 2026-10-18 kernel-type layer rule already forbids (`cross-module-declarations` boundary: compiler-side projections never redeclare); discharging those is the type-name waves H1 to H5, not new work."),
      li(text: "**Variant names:** 409 of 33,933 variant names have more than one production home (Refused 10, Signed 8, the scalar families 7 each). Most are disambiguated by the expected type at a construction; the reading cannot say which are not."),
      li(text: "**The seed-tolerance test did not run.** The seed CLI refuses to run outside a cgroup v2 memory envelope (`gunbc.host.memory_envelope`), and this session's container has none; faking the externally-bounded-executor arm was declined as a bypass of that envelope. The command for a fleet host, baseline then with the entry file's import member lists stripped: `gunbc run --dry-run --claim-run --source-root dag --source-root src/v1 --source-root src/v2 --entry src/v2/test/claim/lexical_scope.dag` (29 claims)."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Reading.** Step one (names bind to their one known declaration) is small and concrete: 54 names need a home in the native-seven closure, about a dozen dual std declarations need their H-wave merge, and the rest of the corpus is one percent ambiguous. Step two (meaning changes: Nat against Int, renamed labels, appended arguments) stays per name in the existing 23-row policy table and is tested the way everything else is, by the claims of the modules that call the name under both compilers."),
    h2(text: "7. Realization"),
    p(text: "Not authored here. The first realization acts are to declare the edges section 2 read from prose and to settle the spelling question; after that, what a landing reports is which legacy mechanism's inventory reached zero, or which binding capability now executes on the native route, never a count of files rewritten."),
  ]
}

data names_without_imports_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly,
  slug: "names-without-imports",
  title: "Names without imports: every name decided one way, and the import concept gone before v2 is accepted",
  body: names_without_imports_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Section 1 is achieved: no import grammar, every reference carrying its declaration through the one kernel, the legacy mechanisms at zero, and native-production-admission consuming it.") },
}
